Fillets and chamfers: an analytic blend whose contact passes over a hole
or a cut-out of its face whose outline is made of several faces (a slot
through a wall, its straight sides planes and its ends half cylinders) is
cut by those faces in turn (mitcad#121). Before (0055, 0057), only a gap
whose contact left its face into a face and came back from the same face
was filled; over other gaps the blend was left to the walking, which
rolled on the hole's edge and failed.

- ChFi3d_FillKPartGaps: where the faces beyond the arcs where the contact
  leaves its face N (B0) and comes back (Bn) differ, ChFi3d_KPartCutChain
  looks for a chain of faces from B0 to Bn (at most four), each sharing an
  edge with the one before that the blend crosses between its contacts,
  at sections in order along the spine (ChFi3d_KPartEdgeCrossing: the
  edge's intersection with the blend's surface, the section through it
  found as for the spine's points). ChFi3d_KPartChainPieces then adds one
  piece per face of the chain (ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece, its ends given at
  the crossings: the point on the edge crossed, with the transition of an
  end on that arc). A face that cuts the blend along a section (both its
  ends on one section: a plane square to a straight spine) gives no
  piece; the vertices of the faces cut where an edge of the chain ends
  under the blend do not refuse the cut.
- On a closed spine, a gap across the spine's start is taken from the end
  of the piece before it in the period before the next piece's start
  (before, its parameters ran backwards and no piece was made).
- ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve: on a closed intersection line one of whose points
  is the line's start or end, the arc round the other way is the rest of
  the line (before, only the direct arc was tried).
- ChFi3d_FilDS: two pieces that share only a part of the section between
  them (one side's points differ, both on edges of one face Bf: the face
  that cuts the blend along that section) get the section up to the
  nearer of the two points, and the rest of it, up to the farther point,
  as an edge between Bf and the piece whose section is the longer, on
  both surfaces (its transition on Bf from the one on the blend, as for
  the contacts); the next piece starts from its own point, which cuts its
  edge.
